-- title:
--   $\max$ of finitely many affine functions is a sum of $n$-order hinges
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $n$ is a fixed dimension and functions are real valued on $\mathbb{R}^n$. An *$n$-order hinge* is a function of the form $x \mapsto \sigma \max_{0 \le i \le n} \ell_i(x)$ with $\sigma \in \{+1,-1\}$ and $\ell_0,\dots,\ell_n$ affine; `IsHH f` means that $f$ is a finite sum of $n$-order hinges. `CPWL` is the class of continuous piecewise linear functions, presented as the sublattice of $\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}^n}$ generated by the affine maps. These notions are supplied by the definition module `WangSunCPWL`.
--
--   **Arity reduction.** The maximum of an arbitrary finite family of affine functions on $\mathbb{R}^n$ is a finite sum of $n$-order hinges: for all $m \ge 0$ and affine $\ell_0,\dots,\ell_m : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}$,
--
--   $$x \;\mapsto\; \max_{0 \le i \le m} \ell_i(x)$$
--
--   satisfies $\mathrm{IsHH}$, that is, it can be written as $\sum_{k=1}^{K} \sigma_k \max_{j \in S_k} \ell_{k,j}$ with every index set $S_k$ of size at most $n+1$.
--
--   For $m \le n$ this is immediate, since a maximum of at most $n+1$ affine arguments is already a hinge (repeat an argument to pad the family to exactly $n+1$). The content is the case $m > n$: a convex piecewise linear function with arbitrarily many pieces must be reassembled from maxima of at most $n+1$ affine functions at the cost of signs and a longer sum. This is the sole point at which the dimension $n$ enters the Wang-Sun theorem, and it is where that theorem's difficulty lies; it is a special case of the full theorem, since a maximum of affine functions is continuous piecewise linear.
-- source:
--   Auxiliary lemma in the decomposition of the Prove2Me theorem WangSun.Main (theorem_id 63eec3c9-377a-4237-8fe6-f5d41ac2d0e2), whose natural-language statement attributes the result to S. Wang and X. Sun, Generalization of hinging hyperplanes, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 51 (2005), 4425-4431. This statement is a step in that decomposition, not a numbered statement of the paper.

import Definitions.Def_WangSunCPWL

open Finset

variable {n : ℕ}

namespace WangSun

theorem sup_affine_isHH {m : ℕ} (L : Fin (m + 1) → ((Fin n → ℝ) →ᵃ[ℝ] ℝ)) :
    IsHH (fun x => (univ : Finset (Fin (m + 1))).sup' univ_nonempty fun i => L i x) := by
  sorry

end WangSun
